  "Number": 1,
  "Question": "If the radius of a circle is a rational number, its area is given by a number which is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "Rational",
    "B": "Irrational",
    "C": "Integral",
    "D": "A perfect square",
    "E": "None of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Let the rational radius be \\(r>0\\). The area is\n\\[\nA=\\pi r^2.\n\\]\nBecause \\(r\\) is rational and nonzero, \\(r^2\\) is also rational and nonzero. If \\(A\\) were rational, then\n\\[\n\\pi=\\frac{A}{r^2}\n\\]\nwould be rational, since a quotient of rational numbers with nonzero denominator is rational. This contradicts the known irrationality of \\(\\pi\\).\n\nTherefore the area is irrational, choice B.",
